-- title:
--   Polynomials whose Laurent coordinates are constant are exactly the polynomials in $F_n$
-- statement:
--   Fix $n\ge4$ and let $A_n=\mathbb{C}[X_0,\dots,X_{n-1}]$. With $h=X_0$, $u=X_1$, $v=X_2$, $w=X_3$, $x=u^3+hv$, $y=-u^2+hw$, $s=2u^3v+3u^4w+h(v^2-3u^2w^2)+h^2w^3$ and $p=-2s^2x+3sy^2-3s^3y$, let $F=h-p-1\in R=\mathbb{C}[X_0,X_1,X_2,X_3]$ and let $F_n\in A_n$ be the same polynomial viewed in $n$ variables. Let $R_{F+1}$ be the localization of $R$ away from $F+1$, let $B_n=R_{F+1}[Y_0,\dots,Y_{n-5}]$, and let $\iota_n:A_n\to B_n$ be the localization map with $\iota_n(X_j)=X_j/1$ for $j\le3$ and $\iota_n(X_{4+i})=Y_i$; it presents $B_n$ as $A_n[(F_n+1)^{-1}]$.
--
--   Let $\mathbb{C}[t,t^{-1}]$ be the ring of Laurent polynomials and let $\Phi_n:B_n\to\mathbb{C}[t,t^{-1}][Z_0,\dots,Z_{n-2}]$ be the explicit $\mathbb{C}$-algebra isomorphism to Laurent coordinates. On coefficients it is the four-variable isomorphism $R_{F+1}\cong\mathbb{C}[t,t^{-1}][Z_0,Z_1,Z_2]$, whose inverse sends $t$ to $F+1$ and $Z_0,Z_1,Z_2$ to $x+s^3$, $y-s^2$ and an explicit element $T$ of $R_{F+1}$; on the extra variables it is $Y_i\mapsto Z_{3+i}$. In particular $\Phi_n(\iota_n(F_n+1))=t$. Then for every polynomial $r\in A_n$,
--
--   $$
--   \Bigl(\exists\,b\in\mathbb{C}[t,t^{-1}]:\quad \Phi_n\bigl(\iota_n(r)\bigr)=b\Bigr)\iff r\in\mathbb{C}[F_n],
--   $$
--
--   where $b$ on the left is regarded as a constant polynomial in $Z_0,\dots,Z_{n-2}$, and $\mathbb{C}[F_n]$ is the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of $A_n$ generated by $F_n$.
--
--   In words: a polynomial whose image in Laurent coordinates involves none of the variables $Z_k$ is exactly a polynomial in $F_n$. Since the common kernel of the partial derivatives $\partial/\partial Z_k$ on $\mathbb{C}[t,t^{-1}][Z_0,\dots,Z_{n-2}]$ is the coefficient ring $\mathbb{C}[t,t^{-1}]$, this identifies the common kernel of the commuting derivations on $A_n$, which are obtained from these partials, with $\mathbb{C}[F_n]$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** In Lean, $\iota_n$ is `extendedLocMap hn`, $\Phi_n$ is `extendedLocalizationEquiv hn`, $\mathbb{C}[t,t^{-1}]$ is `LaurentCoefficients`, defined as `Localization.Away (X : Polynomial ℂ)`, and the constant polynomial is `C b`. The Laurent variables are indexed by `Fin (n-1)` via `blocksToOutput hn`, so that $Z_0,Z_1,Z_2$ are the core coordinates and $Z_{3+i}$ corresponds to $Y_i$.
